WEBVTT

00:30.000 --> 00:32.000
I'm a boy junior.

00:32.000 --> 00:34.000
Where are you going to find a nicer kid than Junior?

00:34.000 --> 00:36.000
Now that's where you left yourself wide open.

00:36.000 --> 00:38.000
You should never have brung up Junior.

00:38.000 --> 00:41.000
Why shouldn't I bring him up? He's my son, isn't he?

00:41.000 --> 00:42.000
Who else is going to bring him up?

00:42.000 --> 00:44.000
I mean bring him up in a conversation.

00:44.000 --> 00:46.000
Your Junior is the weak link in your family.

00:46.000 --> 00:49.000
My Junior is the weak link?

00:49.000 --> 00:50.000
My Junior...

00:50.000 --> 00:51.000
Gillick, you certainly got a nerve.

00:51.000 --> 00:53.000
Give me back my tomato.

00:53.000 --> 00:55.000
Relax Riley, we're only discussing.

00:55.000 --> 00:58.000
You question me with a question, I answer you with an answer.

00:58.000 --> 00:59.000
Your Junior is a nothing.

00:59.000 --> 01:01.000
My Junior is a nothing.

01:01.000 --> 01:05.000
Now if you want to talk about sons, look at my egg bite.

01:05.000 --> 01:09.000
I've looked at your egg bite and you have my deepest sympathies.

01:09.000 --> 01:13.000
Okay, maybe he ain't no Gregory Beck, but my boy has got it up here.

01:13.000 --> 01:17.000
Yeah, well he ought to shampoo more often with kerosene.

01:21.000 --> 01:23.000
What I'm talking about is talent.

01:23.000 --> 01:26.000
If you want your boy to amount to something, you got to watch him when he's young.

01:26.000 --> 01:29.000
Find out what his talent is and nourish it.

01:29.000 --> 01:30.000
Why?

01:30.000 --> 01:31.000
Why?

01:31.000 --> 01:34.000
Because a delicate flower won't grow unless it's fertilized.

01:34.000 --> 01:37.000
My egg bite is a flower and I'm his fertilizer.

01:37.000 --> 01:39.000
Well, my Junior's got plenty of talent.

01:39.000 --> 01:41.000
Yeah? Why?

01:41.000 --> 01:42.000
Well, he...

01:42.000 --> 01:45.000
Well, last summer he swam underwater for five minutes without coming up for air.

01:45.000 --> 01:46.000
Now that's talent, ain't it?

01:46.000 --> 01:49.000
Oh, that's a fine talent, if you're raising a barracuda.

01:49.000 --> 01:52.000
I mean talent for a career.

01:52.000 --> 01:53.000
Oh.

01:53.000 --> 01:55.000
Did you see this?

01:55.000 --> 01:58.000
It's a copy of what they're going to say about my egg bite in the class yearbook.

01:58.000 --> 02:00.000
What class yearbook?

02:00.000 --> 02:02.000
Didn't your Junior show you his?

02:02.000 --> 02:04.000
Oh, I can guess why.

02:04.000 --> 02:06.000
Don't worry, he'll show it to me.

02:06.000 --> 02:08.000
He just probably forgot, that's all.

02:08.000 --> 02:09.000
Let me read you this.

02:09.000 --> 02:10.000
Egg bite killers.

02:10.000 --> 02:13.000
Activities, president of the chemistry club.

02:13.000 --> 02:15.000
Best subject, chemistry.

02:15.000 --> 02:17.000
Prizes, aluminum medal for chemistry.

02:17.000 --> 02:19.000
Hobby, chemistry.

02:19.000 --> 02:22.000
And guess what it says on the future profession.

02:22.000 --> 02:23.000
Unemployed.

02:23.000 --> 02:24.000
Yeah?

02:24.000 --> 02:26.000
Chemical engineer.

02:29.000 --> 02:31.000
Junior, did you find that stuff for your yearbook yet?

02:31.000 --> 02:33.000
Yeah, Pop, I just found it.

02:33.000 --> 02:35.000
I don't understand you, Riley.

02:35.000 --> 02:36.000
You're not reasonable.

02:36.000 --> 02:38.000
For months you don't take the slightest interest in Junior's school.

02:38.000 --> 02:40.000
Now all of a sudden you go overboard.

02:40.000 --> 02:42.000
You want our son Peg to have a future, don't you?

02:42.000 --> 02:44.000
Oh, of course I do.

02:44.000 --> 02:47.000
All right, I want to see his future on paper.

02:48.000 --> 02:50.000
Here it is, Pop.

02:50.000 --> 02:52.000
The page proof is in my briefcase.

02:52.000 --> 02:54.000
All right, let's see.

02:54.000 --> 02:55.000
Here we are.

02:55.000 --> 02:57.000
Chester A. Riley, Junior.

02:57.000 --> 02:59.000
Activities.

03:01.000 --> 03:03.000
Substitute cheerleader?

03:03.000 --> 03:04.000
Cheerleader?

03:04.000 --> 03:05.000
Yeah, watch this.

03:05.000 --> 03:06.000
Squash him like grapes.

03:06.000 --> 03:07.000
Pick up the pits.

03:07.000 --> 03:09.000
Three cheers for John J. Boskowitz.

03:09.000 --> 03:11.000
Team, team, team.

03:13.000 --> 03:14.000
Cheerleader.

03:14.000 --> 03:17.000
For that I paid 50 bucks to have his counsels taken out.

03:17.000 --> 03:19.000
Oh, I think cheerleaders are swell.

03:19.000 --> 03:21.000
Gee whiz, Pop, what's the matter?

03:21.000 --> 03:23.000
All right, we'll let that pass.

03:23.000 --> 03:25.000
Let's look up future professions.

03:25.000 --> 03:26.000
Here it is.

03:26.000 --> 03:28.000
Future professions.

03:28.000 --> 03:30.000
Question mark.

03:30.000 --> 03:31.000
Well, I don't know.

03:31.000 --> 03:33.000
What do you mean you don't know?

03:33.000 --> 03:34.000
Oh, be reasonable, Riley.

03:34.000 --> 03:36.000
Simply means he hasn't made up his mind yet.

03:36.000 --> 03:38.000
Well, he's got to make up his mind.

03:38.000 --> 03:39.000
He's 13 years old.

03:39.000 --> 03:42.000
All he's got on his mind now is movies, movies, and television.

03:42.000 --> 03:45.000
And what did you have on your mind when you were 13 years old?

03:45.000 --> 03:47.000
When I was 13, Peg.

03:48.000 --> 03:50.000
All right, I admit I was a little wild.

03:50.000 --> 03:52.000
And you were right not to elope with me.

03:52.000 --> 03:53.000
Ow!

03:53.000 --> 03:55.000
Gee, you've got sharp teeth.

03:55.000 --> 03:56.000
Come here, son.

03:56.000 --> 03:57.000
Sit down.

04:00.000 --> 04:01.000
Now look, son.

04:02.000 --> 04:04.000
Isn't there something that you'd like to do?

04:04.000 --> 04:06.000
Gosh, I don't know.

04:06.000 --> 04:08.000
Well, you've got to want to be something.

04:08.000 --> 04:10.000
Ain't I right, Peg?

04:10.000 --> 04:13.000
You will one of these days.

04:14.000 --> 04:17.000
Well, isn't there some kind of work you'd like to do?

04:17.000 --> 04:20.000
Well, I wouldn't mind a job like yours, I guess.

04:21.000 --> 04:22.000
That's fine.

04:22.000 --> 04:24.000
That's just dandy.

04:24.000 --> 04:27.000
I can hear the horse laugh I'll get when I get up at that school meeting and say,

04:27.000 --> 04:29.000
my son wants to be like me.

04:29.000 --> 04:30.000
What school meeting?

04:30.000 --> 04:31.000
And that's another thing.

04:31.000 --> 04:34.000
Why didn't you tell me about the father and son meeting?

04:34.000 --> 04:36.000
Well, I, um...

04:36.000 --> 04:38.000
Oh, you just forgot, wasn't that it, dear?

04:38.000 --> 04:39.000
Yeah, that's right.

04:39.000 --> 04:40.000
No.

04:40.000 --> 04:42.000
I just didn't think Pop would want to go.

04:42.000 --> 04:44.000
What do you mean I wouldn't want to go?

04:44.000 --> 04:46.000
All the other fathers are going, ain't they?

04:46.000 --> 04:48.000
They're going to get up and talk about their sons.

04:48.000 --> 04:50.000
I'm just as big a show-off as they are, ain't I?

04:50.000 --> 04:52.000
You certainly are, dear.

04:52.000 --> 04:53.000
Maybe bigger.

04:53.000 --> 04:55.000
Thanks, Peg, for backing me up.

04:55.000 --> 04:56.000
Now work, son.

04:56.000 --> 04:57.000
Excuse me, Pop.

04:57.000 --> 04:59.000
I've got to go finish my homework.

05:03.000 --> 05:05.000
Squash them like grapes.

05:05.000 --> 05:07.000
Pick up the seeds.

05:07.000 --> 05:09.000
Three cheers for John J. Baskowitz.

05:09.000 --> 05:11.000
Why, it don't even rhyme.

05:11.000 --> 05:12.000
Never mind, dear.

05:12.000 --> 05:13.000
It isn't that serious.

05:13.000 --> 05:14.000
He's still a baby.

05:14.000 --> 05:17.000
There's plenty of time for him to make up his mind later on when he goes to college.

05:17.000 --> 05:19.000
There is not time, Peg.

05:19.000 --> 05:20.000
It's getting late.

05:20.000 --> 05:23.000
I've got to know what his talent is so I can start building it up.

05:23.000 --> 05:27.000
Look, for instance, suppose he wanted to be an artist.

05:27.000 --> 05:28.000
All right.

05:28.000 --> 05:30.000
So then I give him some paints and a brush.

05:30.000 --> 05:32.000
Or maybe he wants to be a writer.

05:32.000 --> 05:34.000
So I give him a pad and pencil.

05:34.000 --> 05:35.000
Or he wants to be a banker.

05:35.000 --> 05:37.000
And I give him...

05:37.000 --> 05:39.000
I don't want him to be a banker.

05:39.000 --> 05:42.000
You make up his mind when the time comes.

05:42.000 --> 05:46.000
Maybe, you know, Peg, talent runs in families.

05:46.000 --> 05:48.000
Let's see now.

05:48.000 --> 05:51.000
Whoever amounted to anything on my side of the family.

05:56.000 --> 05:58.000
Let's go to your side of the family.

05:58.000 --> 06:00.000
Well, I had a cousin who wanted to.

06:00.000 --> 06:01.000
He was a great surgeon.

06:01.000 --> 06:02.000
No kidding.

06:02.000 --> 06:03.000
Yeah.

06:03.000 --> 06:04.000
James Prose.

06:04.000 --> 06:05.000
He's very famous.

06:05.000 --> 06:06.000
Maybe Junior takes after him.

06:06.000 --> 06:07.000
Oh, don't be silly.

06:07.000 --> 06:08.000
Why not?

06:08.000 --> 06:09.000
Surgery.

06:09.000 --> 06:10.000
That could be his talent.

06:10.000 --> 06:11.000
Only it's hidden.

06:11.000 --> 06:13.000
But if it's there, I'll find it.

06:13.000 --> 06:16.000
What are you going to do, have Junior take out your appendix?

06:16.000 --> 06:18.000
Now, that's a little ridiculous, Peg.

06:18.000 --> 06:20.000
You know I had my appendix out a year ago.

06:20.000 --> 06:22.000
But you still got yours.

06:22.000 --> 06:24.000
Oh, now, Riley, wait a minute.

06:24.000 --> 06:25.000
I mean...

06:25.000 --> 06:28.000
Well, I'll think of something.

06:28.000 --> 06:30.000
Yeah, I'll give you something to think about, dear.

06:30.000 --> 06:31.000
Something.

06:34.000 --> 06:38.000
Junior, a father's got a duty to his son.

06:38.000 --> 06:41.000
So when I butt in and try to tell you what to do...

06:41.000 --> 06:42.000
Here we are.

06:42.000 --> 06:43.000
Mmm.

06:43.000 --> 06:46.000
Well, my favorite dish, roast chicken.

06:46.000 --> 06:47.000
Now, you can go ahead and carve, dear.

06:47.000 --> 06:48.000
With pleasure.

06:48.000 --> 06:50.000
Here's a knife, Tom.

06:50.000 --> 06:51.000
Thank you.

06:51.000 --> 06:54.000
Uh, wait a minute.

06:54.000 --> 06:56.000
What's the matter?

06:56.000 --> 06:57.000
What are you staring at me for?

06:57.000 --> 06:59.000
Peg, look at Junior's hands.

06:59.000 --> 07:00.000
Hold them up.

07:00.000 --> 07:01.000
I washed them.

07:01.000 --> 07:03.000
I don't mean that.

07:03.000 --> 07:05.000
Look at his fingers, Peg.

07:05.000 --> 07:08.000
So long and slender and delicate.

07:08.000 --> 07:10.000
Go on and carve, will you, Riley?

07:10.000 --> 07:11.000
Please, Peg.

07:11.000 --> 07:14.000
Junior, you carve.

07:14.000 --> 07:15.000
Me?

07:15.000 --> 07:16.000
Oh, now, Riley.

07:16.000 --> 07:17.000
Go ahead, Doctor.

07:17.000 --> 07:18.000
Junior.

07:18.000 --> 07:19.000
Okay.

07:19.000 --> 07:21.000
I always wanted to carve.

07:21.000 --> 07:23.000
You always wanted to carve?

07:23.000 --> 07:24.000
You see, Peg, I was right.

07:24.000 --> 07:27.000
Grab a hold of that scalpel and start operating, Doctor.

07:27.000 --> 07:28.000
Okay, Nurse.

07:28.000 --> 07:29.000
What do you have, Mom?

07:29.000 --> 07:31.000
I'll take a leg, dear.

07:31.000 --> 07:32.000
And I'll have an arm.

07:32.000 --> 07:34.000
I mean, one arm.

07:34.000 --> 07:35.000
I mean a wing.

07:35.000 --> 07:37.000
You take white meat for yourself, Junior.

07:37.000 --> 07:38.000
Okay.

07:38.000 --> 07:40.000
Well, here goes.

07:48.000 --> 07:50.000
Gee, this chicken's tough.

07:50.000 --> 07:51.000
It is not.

07:51.000 --> 07:52.000
It's like butter.

07:56.000 --> 07:58.000
Junior, don't chop at the leg like that.

07:58.000 --> 08:01.000
If that was a human being on that platter, he'd resent it.

08:01.000 --> 08:03.000
I guess I'm not very good at this.

08:03.000 --> 08:04.000
Oh, no, Doctor.

08:04.000 --> 08:05.000
You are good.

08:05.000 --> 08:06.000
You are good.

08:06.000 --> 08:07.000
You're very good.

08:07.000 --> 08:08.000
Go ahead.

08:08.000 --> 08:09.000
A little encouragement.

08:15.000 --> 08:17.000
Here's the leg for you, Mom.

08:17.000 --> 08:18.000
Thank you, dear.

08:22.000 --> 08:25.000
And here's a wing for you, Pop.

08:25.000 --> 08:27.000
The wing?

08:27.000 --> 08:30.000
You call this the wing?

08:30.000 --> 08:35.000
This is the fine surgeon you turned out to be.

08:35.000 --> 08:36.000
No direction.

08:47.000 --> 08:48.000
Some surgeon.

08:48.000 --> 08:50.000
The way he was tearing at that poor chicken.

08:50.000 --> 08:52.000
Oh, forget it, will you?

08:52.000 --> 08:53.000
That's all you keep harping about.

08:53.000 --> 08:54.000
I can't forget it.

08:54.000 --> 08:56.000
A father's got a duty to his kid.

08:56.000 --> 08:58.000
I'm going to make something of that boy

08:58.000 --> 09:00.000
if I have to.

09:00.000 --> 09:01.000
Where is he anyway?

09:01.000 --> 09:02.000
Now, you leave him alone.

09:02.000 --> 09:05.000
He's in his room reading.

09:05.000 --> 09:07.000
Some son we got in his room reading.

09:07.000 --> 09:12.000
He's got some new book about big game hunting in Africa.

09:12.000 --> 09:14.000
No kidding.

09:14.000 --> 09:15.000
He's interested in that?

09:15.000 --> 09:16.000
I guess so.

09:16.000 --> 09:18.000
He's had his nose in that book for about a week.

09:18.000 --> 09:20.000
Hey, Peg.

09:20.000 --> 09:21.000
That's it.

09:21.000 --> 09:22.000
That's this Tyler.

09:22.000 --> 09:23.000
A big game hunter.

09:23.000 --> 09:24.000
He'll be a Frank Buck.

09:24.000 --> 09:26.000
Oh, for heaven's sake.

09:26.000 --> 09:28.000
That's even better than being a doctor.

09:28.000 --> 09:30.000
You'll bring him back alive.

09:30.000 --> 09:32.000
Stop dreaming, dear, will you?

09:32.000 --> 09:34.000
Gee, Peg, I can see it all now.

09:34.000 --> 09:36.000
He's a big game hunter.

09:36.000 --> 09:38.000
Junior's got a safari.

09:38.000 --> 09:40.000
They're crossing a steaming desert

09:40.000 --> 09:42.000
trying to get over a river.

09:42.000 --> 09:44.000
They're chopping at the underbrush.

09:44.000 --> 09:45.000
And they're stalking elephants.

09:45.000 --> 09:47.000
They're trapping lions.

09:47.000 --> 09:50.000
And they're shooting tigers.

09:50.000 --> 09:52.000
Peg, that takes guts.

09:52.000 --> 09:54.000
That takes real courage.

09:54.000 --> 09:56.000
Help, help, Mom!

09:56.000 --> 09:57.000
That's Junior!

09:57.000 --> 09:59.000
Come on, come on, Peg.

09:59.000 --> 10:00.000
Help, Mom, come on.

10:00.000 --> 10:01.000
Help!

10:01.000 --> 10:02.000
Help!

10:02.000 --> 10:03.000
Junior, what's the matter?

10:03.000 --> 10:04.000
What's the matter?

10:04.000 --> 10:05.000
There's a mouse in my room.

10:09.000 --> 10:11.000
Case why.

10:11.000 --> 10:14.000
Patient was 13-year-old boy

10:14.000 --> 10:18.000
suffering from complex new roses

10:18.000 --> 10:21.000
during adolescence.

10:21.000 --> 10:24.000
Well, Junior's 13.

10:24.000 --> 10:30.000
Chief symptoms, indecision, vaccination,

10:30.000 --> 10:35.000
vacillation, sense of inferiority,

10:35.000 --> 10:37.000
lack of interest.

10:37.000 --> 10:40.000
That's Junior's case exactly.

10:40.000 --> 10:43.000
Analysis revealed basic cause

10:43.000 --> 10:48.000
to be an octopus complex.

10:48.000 --> 10:52.000
And a deep-rooted hatred of his father.

10:52.000 --> 10:53.000
Oh, no.

10:53.000 --> 10:55.000
He hates me.

10:55.000 --> 10:59.000
How can he hate me when I'm so lovable?

10:59.000 --> 11:02.000
However, complete cure was affected

11:02.000 --> 11:06.000
by inducing patient to talk freely

11:06.000 --> 11:10.000
and frankly admit his father hatred.

11:10.000 --> 11:12.000
Once this mental block was removed,

11:12.000 --> 11:15.000
patient became normal and adjusted.

11:15.000 --> 11:18.000
And today is outstanding lawyer.

11:18.000 --> 11:20.000
Outstanding lawyer.

11:20.000 --> 11:22.000
Imagine that.

11:22.000 --> 11:26.000
Just because the kid admitted he hated his father.

11:26.000 --> 11:27.000
Hey, Junior.

11:27.000 --> 11:28.000
Junior, come here quick.

11:31.000 --> 11:32.000
You called me, Pop?

11:32.000 --> 11:36.000
Junior, how would you like to be an outstanding lawyer?

11:36.000 --> 11:37.000
A lawyer?

11:37.000 --> 11:39.000
Yeah, that'd be keen.

11:39.000 --> 11:41.000
OK, lay down on the couch.

11:41.000 --> 11:42.000
Huh?

11:42.000 --> 11:44.000
I said lay down on the couch.

11:44.000 --> 11:45.000
I'm not tired.

11:45.000 --> 11:46.000
Junior, I'm your father.

11:46.000 --> 11:47.000
Do as I say.

11:47.000 --> 11:49.000
Lay down on the couch.

11:49.000 --> 11:50.000
OK, I'll lie down.

11:57.000 --> 11:59.000
Now tell me, son.

11:59.000 --> 12:00.000
Who's your father?

12:00.000 --> 12:01.000
My what?

12:01.000 --> 12:02.000
Now don't get up.

12:02.000 --> 12:03.000
Don't get up.

12:03.000 --> 12:04.000
Stay right there.

12:04.000 --> 12:05.000
Just tell me who your father is.

12:05.000 --> 12:07.000
Why, you are.

12:07.000 --> 12:09.000
Now we're getting someplace.

12:09.000 --> 12:12.000
Uh, how do you feel about me, Junior?

12:12.000 --> 12:13.000
How do I feel?

12:13.000 --> 12:15.000
Yeah, you hate me, don't you?

12:15.000 --> 12:16.000
Who, me?

12:16.000 --> 12:17.000
Of course not.

12:17.000 --> 12:18.000
Just stay there, Junior.

12:18.000 --> 12:19.000
Come on, now.

12:19.000 --> 12:20.000
Come on, admit it.

12:20.000 --> 12:21.000
You hate me.

12:21.000 --> 12:22.000
I don't hate you.

12:22.000 --> 12:23.000
Don't be stubborn, Junior.

12:23.000 --> 12:25.000
Before I'm finished with you, you'll hate me.

12:25.000 --> 12:26.000
Oh, come on, Pop.

12:26.000 --> 12:27.000
Will you let me out of here?

12:27.000 --> 12:28.000
Will you please stay there?

12:28.000 --> 12:30.000
You're not getting up until you're cured.

12:30.000 --> 12:31.000
Now come on, talk freely.

12:31.000 --> 12:32.000
Admit it.

12:32.000 --> 12:33.000
You hate me.

12:33.000 --> 12:35.000
Say you hate me, and you'll make me the happiest man in the world.

12:35.000 --> 12:36.000
Well, Pop, I don't hate you.

12:36.000 --> 12:37.000
I love you.

12:37.000 --> 12:38.000
You don't love me.

12:38.000 --> 12:39.000
You hate me.

12:39.000 --> 12:43.000
You know what you're saying, Junior?

12:43.000 --> 12:45.000
I'm not doing anything.

12:45.000 --> 12:47.000
I'm not doing anything.

12:47.000 --> 12:48.000
Junior.

12:48.000 --> 12:50.000
Oh, Junior, you're hurting me.

12:50.000 --> 12:54.000
What is the meaning of this?

12:54.000 --> 12:56.000
Peg, I got some horrible news for you,

12:56.000 --> 12:58.000
and we might as well face it.

12:58.000 --> 13:00.000
Junior loves me.

13:00.000 --> 13:02.000
He's a hopeless case.

13:02.000 --> 13:06.000
Is that you dear?

13:06.000 --> 13:07.000
Hello, Peg.

13:07.000 --> 13:08.000
Oh, hello, dear.

13:08.000 --> 13:10.200
Listen, you leave him alone, Dr. Freud.

13:10.200 --> 13:13.240
He's my boy too, and I'm not worried about his future.

13:13.240 --> 13:15.240
Oh, Peggy, here's another carrot.

13:15.240 --> 13:19.000
Gillis couldn't finish my lunch.

13:19.000 --> 13:22.760
Peg, what am I going to say tonight at the school meeting?

13:22.760 --> 13:24.500
What you always say at the school meeting,

13:24.500 --> 13:26.560
wake me up when it's over.

13:26.560 --> 13:27.740
But I haven't got a speech.

13:27.740 --> 13:29.400
All the other fathers will have speeches,

13:29.400 --> 13:30.600
and I'll be there speechless.

13:30.600 --> 13:31.060
So what?

13:31.060 --> 13:32.600
Now you go on and get dressed, and then we'll have supper,

13:32.600 --> 13:35.240
and we'll get over to the school.

13:35.240 --> 13:36.700
I ain't going.

13:36.700 --> 13:37.740
You're not going?

13:37.740 --> 13:39.000
No.

13:39.000 --> 13:41.000
Well, all right.

13:41.000 --> 13:42.700
I can't make you go.

13:42.700 --> 13:45.700
Guess I'll just have to go alone with Junior.

13:45.700 --> 13:46.640
Oh, say, Mom.

13:46.640 --> 13:47.960
Oh, hi, Pop.

13:47.960 --> 13:50.160
Say, Mom, could I have two spools of thread and a needle

13:50.160 --> 13:51.160
and a pair of scissors?

13:51.160 --> 13:53.100
Yeah, it's in my sewing basket in Bab's room,

13:53.100 --> 13:54.200
but what do you want with it?

13:54.200 --> 13:55.640
I got a swell idea to make something.

13:59.000 --> 14:01.360
Ah, son.

14:01.360 --> 14:03.700
Instead of taking chemicals and inventing something,

14:03.700 --> 14:06.240
he's playing around with a needle and thread.

14:06.240 --> 14:08.160
Well, we're raising a tailor.

14:08.160 --> 14:09.500
Fine profession.

14:09.500 --> 14:11.060
What's wrong with being a tailor?

14:11.060 --> 14:13.060
What's wrong with it?

14:13.060 --> 14:14.900
Hey, that's right.

14:14.900 --> 14:17.360
There's nothing wrong with it.

14:17.360 --> 14:19.400
Only not a tailor, Peg.

14:19.400 --> 14:20.540
A dress designer.

14:20.540 --> 14:21.560
Maybe that's his talent.

14:21.560 --> 14:23.000
Oh, now don't start in again.

14:23.000 --> 14:25.340
First you had him a surgeon, then a big game hunter.

14:25.340 --> 14:26.500
Now a designer.

14:26.500 --> 14:28.600
Peg, I got a feeling that this is it.

14:28.600 --> 14:29.600
A dress designer.

14:29.600 --> 14:32.660
Why, there's a fortune in it.

14:32.660 --> 14:35.900
Gee, he'll have a saloon in Paris, a saloon in New York.

14:35.900 --> 14:37.700
Saloon.

14:37.700 --> 14:38.940
And just think, Peg.

14:38.940 --> 14:41.160
When all them society debutantes come out,

14:41.160 --> 14:44.500
they'll be coming out of his dresses.

14:44.500 --> 14:46.940
I can see it all now, Peg.

14:46.940 --> 14:49.240
We're visiting him in Paris.

14:49.240 --> 14:51.700
There's a big sign with his name on it.

14:51.700 --> 14:53.400
Our son.

14:53.400 --> 14:57.100
Madam Junior Riley.

14:57.100 --> 14:58.240
What a weird idea.

14:58.240 --> 14:59.440
Come down to earth, will you?

14:59.440 --> 15:00.960
Just because he asked for a needle and thread

15:00.960 --> 15:02.000
doesn't mean that he's got a...

15:02.000 --> 15:03.700
No, Peg, I got a hunch that this is it.

15:03.700 --> 15:06.100
That boy of mine has found himself at last.

15:06.100 --> 15:07.540
A dress designer.

15:07.540 --> 15:10.040
Come on, Peg, let's go see what the little genius has created.

15:10.040 --> 15:11.760
Oh, Riley, I got supper to get on.

15:11.760 --> 15:14.900
Come on, Peg, this is more important than supper.

15:14.900 --> 15:16.360
Wait till Gillis hears about this.

15:16.360 --> 15:22.100
Come on, Peg, come on.

15:22.100 --> 15:23.200
Well, Madam Junior.

15:23.200 --> 15:24.000
Huh?

15:24.000 --> 15:25.960
Show your mother and me your creation.

15:25.960 --> 15:27.040
My what?

15:27.040 --> 15:27.840
Your creation.

15:27.840 --> 15:29.640
What you made with the needle and thread.

15:29.640 --> 15:30.700
Oh, no.

15:30.700 --> 15:33.040
Come on, dear, don't be so modest.

15:33.040 --> 15:40.900
Well, here it is.

15:40.900 --> 15:42.060
What is it?

15:42.060 --> 15:46.460
It's a yo-yo.

15:46.460 --> 15:50.200
Our little genius created a yo-yo.

15:50.200 --> 15:54.500
You know, Peg, it takes some men 50 years to become a failure.

15:54.500 --> 16:00.440
Junior made it in 13.

16:00.440 --> 16:13.260
And so, in summing up, I leave you parents and sons with this thought.

16:13.260 --> 16:21.100
My 30 years as a junior high school teacher have convinced me of the truth of the old saying.

16:21.100 --> 16:24.500
The boy is father to the man.

16:24.500 --> 16:29.340
Teacher's a wonderful speaker, isn't he, Junior?

16:29.340 --> 16:30.340
Yeah, he's okay.

16:30.340 --> 16:31.940
He's a good teacher.

16:31.940 --> 16:34.340
Hey, there's Pop.

16:34.340 --> 16:37.340
Oh, Riley, I'm so glad you came, dear.

16:37.340 --> 16:38.140
Hello, Peg.

16:38.140 --> 16:39.240
Hello, Junior.

16:39.240 --> 16:45.040
And now, we're going to hear from another proud father, Mr. James Madison Gillis.

16:45.040 --> 16:50.040
Oh.

16:50.040 --> 16:53.740
Peg, I hope they don't ask me to get up and make a speech.

16:53.740 --> 17:04.140
I'll be right back.

17:04.140 --> 17:11.140
Mr. Chairman, fellow fathers and mothers, I'm sure we just all enjoyed the very cute

17:11.140 --> 17:13.340
little speech the teacher just gave us.

17:13.340 --> 17:15.740
But now, we come to something interesting.

17:15.740 --> 17:18.540
Now, I ain't the kind of father likes to boast about his kid.

17:18.540 --> 17:21.040
I believe that actions speak louder than words.

17:21.040 --> 17:25.940
So now, my son, Egbert, will perform one of his very unusual chemical experiments right

17:25.940 --> 17:27.540
before your very eyes.

17:27.540 --> 17:31.740
Egbert?

17:31.740 --> 17:36.340
Now, darling, tell all these sweet people, what are you going to invent with that chemical

17:36.340 --> 17:37.340
set?

17:37.340 --> 17:39.940
Well, I think I'll make NACL.

17:39.940 --> 17:41.340
Did you hear that, folks?

17:41.340 --> 17:44.640
He's going to make knackle.

17:44.640 --> 17:47.140
Gee, Junior, why didn't you have a knack for making knackle?

17:47.140 --> 17:49.440
Okay, Egbert, the floor is yours.

17:49.440 --> 17:51.140
I'm not quite ready yet, Papa.

17:51.140 --> 17:52.140
Oh.

17:52.140 --> 17:56.340
Well, while we're waiting, we can waste a little time by listening to one of the other

17:56.340 --> 17:57.340
fathers.

17:57.340 --> 18:03.140
Oh, how about my good friend Riley?

18:03.140 --> 18:04.540
Come on, folks.

18:04.540 --> 18:05.540
Let's give him a nice big hand.

18:05.540 --> 18:06.540
He's a little shy.

18:06.540 --> 18:07.540
No, no.

18:07.540 --> 18:10.540
Come on, Riley, don't be so modest.

18:10.540 --> 18:12.740
Tell all the folks about your junior's future.

18:12.740 --> 18:13.740
I can.

18:13.740 --> 18:14.740
I'm keeping it a secret.

18:14.740 --> 18:15.740
Riley, go on.

18:15.740 --> 18:16.740
Say something.

18:16.740 --> 18:17.740
Everybody's staring.

18:17.740 --> 18:18.740
Sure.

18:18.740 --> 18:19.740
Hey, huh?

18:19.740 --> 18:20.740
They're all yours.

18:20.740 --> 18:21.740
How are you?

18:21.740 --> 18:22.740
Excuse me.

18:22.740 --> 18:42.140
Oh, well, good evening, ladies and gentlemen, Mr. Chairman, fellow fathers and fellow mothers.

18:42.140 --> 18:47.980
I've been studying my junior for quite some time now, and for a while there, we thought

18:47.980 --> 18:58.980
he was going to be one of those... And then we found out that he wasn't going to be that.

18:58.980 --> 19:06.020
And then we found out that he had some talent for... Well, he was starting to make those

19:06.020 --> 19:11.340
... Then we found out he didn't have any talent for that.

19:11.340 --> 19:16.180
So then we went out and we bought him one of those... It was a large.

19:16.180 --> 19:22.100
They had him over in that... Then he wanted a needle and thread.

19:22.100 --> 19:26.700
He wanted to go big game hunting.

19:26.700 --> 19:31.940
So I now give you my wife's son, Junior.

19:31.940 --> 19:34.140
Mom, I can't make a speech.

19:34.140 --> 19:35.140
Go on, dear.

19:35.140 --> 19:36.140
Say something.

19:36.140 --> 19:37.140
You'll be fine.

19:37.140 --> 19:40.060
Pop, I can't make a speech.

19:40.060 --> 19:41.060
Why not?

19:41.060 --> 19:42.060
I just made a lovely speech.

19:42.060 --> 19:43.060
Papa, I'm ready now.

19:43.060 --> 19:44.060
Well, my head is ready now.

19:44.060 --> 19:45.060
Okay, Riley.

19:45.060 --> 19:46.060
Sit down.

19:46.060 --> 19:47.060
Go ahead, Eggbite, my dear boy.

19:47.060 --> 19:48.060
And if you're test tube here, I have some hydrochloric acid here.

19:48.060 --> 19:49.060
You hear that, folks?

19:49.060 --> 19:50.060
Hydrocaloric acid.

19:50.060 --> 19:51.060
Go ahead, my little darling.

19:51.060 --> 19:52.060
This is a match.

19:52.060 --> 19:53.060
You got to admit, Peg, he is clever.

19:53.060 --> 20:13.620
I now take care of this alcohol lamp here.

20:13.620 --> 20:17.340
And heat the contents of this test tube here.

20:17.340 --> 20:30.060
Observe how it burns with a bright... Eggbite, let me look at you.

20:30.060 --> 20:31.060
What happened to your eyebrows?

20:31.060 --> 20:32.060
Oh, my poor little agent.

20:32.060 --> 20:33.060
He lost his eyebrows.

20:33.060 --> 20:34.060
Papa, my eye got burnt.

20:34.060 --> 20:35.060
Ow, my hand.

20:35.060 --> 20:36.060
Get a doctor in the house.

20:36.060 --> 20:37.060
Get a doctor quick.

20:37.060 --> 20:38.060
I know what to do.

20:38.060 --> 20:39.060
I know what to do.

20:39.060 --> 20:40.060
You'll be okay.

20:40.060 --> 20:41.060
Hold out your hand.

20:41.060 --> 20:42.060
Put this tannic acid on.

20:42.060 --> 20:43.060
That'll do it.

20:43.060 --> 20:44.060
Here, it'll be okay in a second, now.

20:44.060 --> 20:45.060
Oh, that feels good, Junior.

20:45.060 --> 20:46.060
Here, here.

20:46.060 --> 20:47.060
I saw it.

20:47.060 --> 20:48.060
I'm a doctor.

20:48.060 --> 20:49.060
Let me look at that boy's hand.

20:49.060 --> 20:50.060
I put some tannic acid on the doctor.

20:50.060 --> 20:51.060
Well, that's exactly what I would have done myself.

20:51.060 --> 20:52.060
That's quick thinking, my boy.

20:52.060 --> 20:53.060
Someday, you'll be a great doctor.

20:53.060 --> 20:54.060
A doctor?

20:54.060 --> 20:55.060
I'm going to sue the Board of Education for this.

20:55.060 --> 20:56.060
But, Papa, I'm all right now.

20:56.060 --> 20:57.060
Are you with me or against me?

20:57.060 --> 20:58.060
You're not all right, and shut up.

20:58.060 --> 20:59.060
I'm going to sue.

20:59.060 --> 21:00.060
That lamp, there must have been something wrong with that lamp.

21:00.060 --> 21:01.060
I'll sue.

21:01.060 --> 21:02.060
You can't sue, Mr. Gillis.

21:02.060 --> 21:03.060
It was Eggbert's own lamp.

21:03.060 --> 21:04.060
The school's going to sue you.

21:04.060 --> 21:13.060
The boy's right, Mr. Gillis.

21:13.060 --> 21:14.060
You have no case here.

21:14.060 --> 21:15.060
How?

21:15.060 --> 21:16.060
What do you know about this?

21:16.060 --> 21:17.060
Well, aside from being the teacher, I also happen to be a lawyer.

21:17.060 --> 21:18.060
A lawyer?

21:18.060 --> 21:19.060
Well, we'll see about this.

21:19.060 --> 21:20.060
I'm taking this case to court.

21:20.060 --> 21:21.060
I'll bet you lose, Mr. Gillis.

21:21.060 --> 21:22.060
I'll bet you five to one.

21:22.060 --> 21:23.060
Hey, did you hear that?

21:23.060 --> 21:24.060
I'll bet you five to one.

21:24.060 --> 21:25.060
Ladies and gentlemen.

21:25.060 --> 21:26.060
Ladies and gentlemen.

21:26.060 --> 21:27.060
I'm going to sue the Board of Education for this.

21:27.060 --> 21:28.060
Now, I'm going to sue.

21:28.060 --> 21:29.060
I'm going to sue.

21:29.060 --> 21:30.060
Come on, Mr. Gillis.

21:30.060 --> 21:32.460
Ladies and gentlemen, ladies and gentlemen,

21:32.460 --> 21:34.460
I know what my son's gonna be.

21:34.460 --> 21:38.460
He's gonna be a doctor, a lawyer, and a bookmaker.

21:38.460 --> 21:40.260
Ah!

21:40.260 --> 21:43.460
Then I'm mighty proud of you, as I always was.

21:43.460 --> 21:56.140
["The!

21:56.140 --> 22:04.340
Ploughman democratically

